WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.100
KELLY O'BRIANT: Today
I'm giving a webinar

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.830
on Interactivity in Production.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:14.320
This webinar is the third
in a three part series

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.050
that we've been doing last
month Garrett Grellman

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.310
delivered Reproducibility in
Production and Thomas mock

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.670
delivered RStudio
Connect in Production.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.530
Those are freely
available on demand

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.010
at resources.rstudio.com
the same place this one will

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.600
be made available, but before
jumping into interactivity

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.930
in production, I wanted to
acknowledge some of the themes

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.970
from those first two
webinars that I'll

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.650
be touching on as well.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.400
The first theme is production
means different things

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.140
to different people.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.330
Garrett talked about
this, Tom talked

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.760
about this really well
with RStudio Connect

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.330
and how the connect product
can meet you where you are

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:59.500
and make production
available in different forms

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.870
with different meanings
to wherever you are

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.959
in your analytics lifecycle.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.310
Also along the
same lines, RStudio

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.320
connectives this
publishing platform

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.110
for communicating impactful
data science work.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.330
R Markdown, is a
tool for creating

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:23.040
computational documents and
communicating impactful data

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.790
science through either static
reports or documents that

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.190
have interactive
elements inside them.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:36.210
And the one thing that I want
to use as a jumping off point

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.370
for starting our
discussion today,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:42.850
is what Garrett introduced in
the reproducibility webinar,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.810
which was the
interactivity spectrum.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.100
So in this
interactivity spectrum,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.860
Garrett made this case
for thinking strategically

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.460
about what level of
interactivity your data product

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.140
actually requires.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.530
And meeting that level of
interactivity with the type of

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.690
experience that you
want to deliver in order

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.030
to communicate effectively.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:10.470
So I really like this a
lot, but it does presuppose

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:15.030
that you know what you want
to build in the first place,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.180
or you're at a point in your
iterative development work

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.740
cycle where you're willing to do
some introspection on what you

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.550
should or could be building.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.550
This is important because
as you progress down

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.630
the interactivity
hierarchy, things become

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.730
more complicated to build.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.030
And we tend to think
of Shiny applications

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.400
the highest level
of interactivity

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.430
as the most complex to develop.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.210
Now, on the other side
of things Garrett also

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.420
spoke to this in his webinar, we
tend to see people learn Shiny,

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.040
and then use it for
just a lot of things.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.690
Borderline on everything.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.330
And I know I've done
this and it's very hard

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:04.200
once you commit to this
Shiny application workflow

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.130
or development cycle to
walk back from Shiny.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.190
In other words, once you've
committed to using Shiny,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.970
it's difficult to take a step
back and evaluate whether you

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.660
would be better served
delivering your desired user

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.460
experience in another format.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.320
Now, all that being
said, I love Shiny.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.590
So I want to talk about the
case for starting with Shiny

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:33.430
because I know that folks
out there do this too,

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:38.450
it's a great tool, it
makes things really easy

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.360
and it's a great tool
for doing a lot of things

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.650
like exploratory data
analysis, prototyping, fast

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:50.060
iteration through building tools
that other folks might want

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.630
to use and that you can use
to drive impact of your data

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.690
science work inside of
your own organization.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.930
Now I know Shiny Wizard,
you can go and look

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:04.730
at the Shiny contest results we
ran last spring to see evidence

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.090
of actual Shiny wizardry,
but I'm comfortable enough

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:11.720
with Shiny that I can build
really powerful prototypes

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.330
in a short amount of time
and I know a lot of folks

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.750
are in that same boat.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.579
So when I need to build a tool
that other people might want

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.160
to use too, my
natural inclination

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.970
is to start developing
that tool in Shiny.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.580
Now these things
can quickly escalate

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.110
when you want to give access to
someone else to use that tool.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.860
And that's what I want
to talk about today.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:44.090
I think of Starting with Shiny
as the equivalent of writing

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:45.740
an hour long talk.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.770
So I call Starting with
Shiny and local development

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.140
as the hour long talk
of data products.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.140
And hour long talks
if you've ever

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:58.160
developed one can be
rambling, cluttered you have

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.670
a lot of time to work with.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.700
So there can be parts
that work really well,

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.340
and parts that
don't work so well.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.860
And I know I'm giving an
hour long talk currently,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.170
so I hope that in
the questions you'll

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.050
let me know which parts
work well and which do not,

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.810
so that I can clarify those.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:19.880
But the goal is when you're
taking this local development

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.670
asset into production,
is that you

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:26.570
want to turn that hour
long talk of data products

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.280
into the lightning
talk of data products.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.060
So the difference between
hour long talks and lightning

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:37.070
talks are lightning talks are
these targeted really elegant

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:41.300
streamlined little
pockets of information

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.930
that you're trying to deliver
in a very short amount of time.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.390
And the irony is
that it can take

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:50.870
a lot more time of your
own to develop a lightning

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.200
talk than it can to
develop an hour long talk.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.300
So you have a lot
of space to work

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.090
with because these
lightning talks they just

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:02.990
need to have that
streamlined, that concision,

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:07.220
you need to get to your message
effectively and conclude

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.650
everything in the
right amount of time.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.240
So maybe you start with
Shiny and local development,

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.480
and by the end
maybe in production

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:23.030
that original Shiny application
you had on your local machine

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:24.350
looks very different.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.550
Maybe once you
get to production,

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.960
it's a group of assets that
complement each other maybe

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.450
it's a very different
kind of Shiny application.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:35.730
As long as you're
evaluating along the way,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.380
starting with Shiny in the
hierarchy of interactivity

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:42.660
is really OK by me.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.790
So this is my agenda for the
webinar discussion today.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:51.740
I started with making a
case for starting with Shiny

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.040
and now I want to
talk about when you're

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.830
taking a prototype of
a Shiny application

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:02.600
into production what I consider
the most important thing is.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:07.550
And this is my perspective as a
solutions engineer at RStudio.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.850
Then I want to talk about Joe
Cheng's most important thing

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:12.470
for shining in production.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:17.180
Joe is, if you aren't
familiar the CTO of RStudio

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:24.470
and he was the creator
of the Shiny package.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.710
After that I want to
talk about an example

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:31.220
a prototype app that I built
and then went through steps

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.620
that Joe outlines in
his performance workflow

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.495
to take that application
into production.

00:07:36.495 --> 00:07:38.120
And along the way
I'm going to show you

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.390
what steps I took to
refactor and then evaluate

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.370
the impact of that application.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:46.940
And finally reconsider the
hierarchy of interactivity

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.170
for what I've produced.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.780
So let's jump right into it.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:56.350
This is what I consider the
most important thing for running

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.140
Shiny in production.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:02.400
And that is you need to
have a sandbox publishing

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.060
environment as part of your
development infrastructure.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.100
This thing right
here, you need it.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.320
It should be a place where
you can publish freely

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.700
into server that is
ideally configured to be

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.910
an identical twin of
your production server.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:22.070
And this is your testing ground.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:27.020
You can call it a sandbox,
staging, test server, whatever.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.860
You need one of these.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.909
If you only have one server
at your disposal in a pinch

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:36.940
even something
like this can work.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.390
You can have the
same piece of content

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.780
be published to two
different destinations

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:50.350
on a single RStudio Connect
box, and have one destination be

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:54.070
reserved for production while
the other destination is

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:57.340
reserved for testing staging.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:01.930
If you need any help
at all in your journey

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:06.820
to production advocating for the
right analytic infrastructure,

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:10.420
please reach out to us
at solutions.rstudio.com

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:11.920
where there are a
bunch of resources

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.570
for having these conversations,
or community.rstudio.com

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:18.010
in the R Admins channel.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.830
And that's where a lot of
us on solutions engineering

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.687
like to hang out
and answer questions

00:09:22.687 --> 00:09:23.770
coming from the community.

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:27.320
So two great resources there.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.860
So that was my most
important thing.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.810
Joe has a very
different perspective

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:33.500
and it's equally valid.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.350
So these are two
awesome opinions

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:39.550
for Joe's Shiny in production
most important thing.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:44.610
He highlights in this excellent
talk that he gave at RStudio

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:48.010
conf 2019, which
is also available

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:54.250
resources.rstudio.com/webinars
for you to watch.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:57.220
This talk is excellent
and this is your homework

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:02.050
from this webinar, is to go
watch Joe's talk if you haven't

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:05.710
seen it already because it is
really that impactful if you

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:09.980
are doing anything that
involves Shiny in production.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.950
So this talk was
the keynote where

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:16.090
he introduced the tools that
are Shiny engineering team here

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.720
at RStudio has been
working on to help support

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:22.690
bringing Shiny into production.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:27.580
So it starts with going through
Shinytest, Shinyloadtest,

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:30.970
Profvis, plot
catching and async.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.910
And he highlighted a
couple of these tools

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.010
throughout his
talk, but the thing

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:40.060
that's most important
to Joe, is Profvis

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:41.470
for Shiny in production.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:44.920
This is a general
profile for our code,

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:50.440
but it's super important for
when you're bringing Shiny

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.880
applications into production.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.770
A lot of prototype applications
fall over in production

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:00.940
because they contain these
performance antipodeans.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:05.440
So it's important
when you're trying

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.550
to turn a prototype into
a production application,

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.880
to ask yourself
questions and suss out

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:17.170
whether your application
is suitable for production

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:18.830
in its current state.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.450
So two questions you can
answer that are great

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:27.320
is, is this app fast
enough for production?

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:33.520
And then if it's not, what is
the cause of that performance

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:35.000
bottleneck?

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.450
So through the course
of Joe's keynote,

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:41.230
he goes through and looks at
an example application called

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:44.890
CRAN Whales that he created.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:51.550
And covered how to make
that application faster

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:54.370
through the application
of these two tools.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:12:00.340
Shinyloadtest to see if the
app is fast enough and Profvis

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:04.570
to see what in the
R code is making

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:07.160
that happen not fast enough.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:12.040
So he lays out this excellent
performance workflow, which

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:15.970
is the real gem
here of this talk,

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:18.270
you ask if the app
is fast enough,

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.500
you test to see
what's making it slow,

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:25.960
and then three is you optimize
you solve those problems.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:30.250
And the importance
of using Profvis,

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.300
is you can tell how passionate
Joe is about this when he talks

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.300
about it and in the
keynote he says,

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:42.220
you cannot guess what is
making your R code slow,

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:47.230
you do have to use this profile
or because your intuition about

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:50.980
what is making that
code slow it stinks,

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:54.730
it's garbage it's not good.

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:57.820
Even folks who are very
competent at building Shiny

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:02.710
applications can get tripped
up by simply assuming

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:04.250
you know what the problem is.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.240
So Profvis, is just
an amazing tool

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:10.750
to help you get to
the optimization stage

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:16.090
and then make real impactful
changes to how your code works.

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:23.400
So in the spirit of Joe's talk
and his CRAN Whales application

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.910
success story I wanted to
build a similar application

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.320
for this webinar.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.230
And I won't repeat the
performance work for a demo

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:35.160
that Joe has already
done and his, definitely

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.220
go watch it it's
really great seriously,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.320
but I wanted to
create this prototype

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:43.050
to start that
would realistically

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:46.950
highlight some of those same
or similar performance anti

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:47.950
patterns.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:54.360
So I have a CRAN Logs app that's
called the trending R packages

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:55.260
app.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:56.850
And here's what it does.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.920
As you can see here
in the screenshot

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:04.440
it has this table
it's dt table and you

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.740
can see which R
packages have been

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:13.560
trending over the last
period of time the top 10.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:18.210
And then click on each
of these applications

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:24.270
to view a little bit of metadata
about what that package is

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.200
and what it does.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:30.900
So all of this data is
coming from the CRAN Logs API

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:37.320
project plus a little bit of
our best magic scraping metadata

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:39.930
about each package.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.820
So the workflow
that it goes through

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:46.890
is that I call out to the
CRAN Logs API slash trending

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:50.160
and point to get those
top 10 trending packages,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:54.690
then I make 10 more calls
to that CRAN Logs API

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:58.110
to retrieve the download
counts for each package.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.890
Put that data into DT
here in the application.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:04.290
And then I use our best
magic to scrape out

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:08.740
that metadata about each
package when at the point

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:09.810
the user clicks on it.

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:19.550
So this app it
makes sense to me.

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:23.200
I'm constructing
this data as I go,

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:26.050
I'm not thinking about
scaling at this point.

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:29.410
Works pretty great in my
local development environment.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:34.180
When I push it to my
staging server it works OK,

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:39.280
but I can already tell that
there is some danger here

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:44.050
as more users come to this
application I'm worried that it

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:47.680
won't be fast enough to
support the number of users

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.090
that I expect.

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.610
So at this point
I think to myself

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:55.660
this would be a great case
to activate Joe's performance

00:15:55.660 --> 00:16:00.430
workflow and move through
Shinyloadtest and Profvis

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:04.060
to get the answers about
whether my application is

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:08.670
going to work in production and
how to fix it so that it does.

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:14.880
So I'll spare you the first
two points of the workflow.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.280
Again go watch just
talk for those.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:23.110
And skip to number three
where I'm optimizing my code.

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:26.160
So at this point,
the number one thing

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.820
that you do very
often to optimize

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.980
some of these
performance issues,

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.980
is to move work out of Shiny.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:38.670
And Joe says there are
two important things

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.640
to consider when you're
moving work out of Shiny.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.090
Things that you
don't want to do.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:45.960
So you don't want
to do work in Shiny

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:48.233
while the user is waiting.

00:16:48.233 --> 00:16:49.650
And the second
thing is, you don't

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:53.160
want to do the same work
for every user that's

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:54.470
very wasteful.

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:57.630
So he had this example
of loading the CSV

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:03.050
data into a Shiny application
every time a user connects.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.819
And this application
I actually designed

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:08.700
for the purpose of highlighting
these two ethic patterns,

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:12.660
but it does a very
similar thing.

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:18.750
I am making 11 API requests to
the trending R packages table

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:22.089
to create that
data for the user.

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:26.520
And then once the user
is on the application,

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:32.190
I'm calling R best
two times to perform

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:37.600
that metadata scraping for
each row Selection that occurs.

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:41.970
So that would be repeating
that same work over and over

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:47.680
again for every user which
again, very wasteful.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:51.070
So how do we solve this problem?

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:55.290
There are a number of ways I
can think of to solve this.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.230
I could generate the
data ahead of time

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:03.300
and publish it in the bundle
along with my application code.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:06.780
And this is a totally
valid workflow

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:10.290
and makes sense to do for a
lot of different types of Shiny

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:10.890
applications.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:15.180
Sometimes you can just pull
the data together and throw it

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:20.550
in that bundle and send it along
as you're deploying to staging

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:23.830
to production and it's fine.

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:26.070
The problem here is
that I want users

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:29.640
to see the latest and
greatest trending R packages.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:33.180
So literally an application
to explore trends.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:37.020
So I don't want to get
stuck manually deploying

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:39.255
that new data or
cobbling together

00:18:39.255 --> 00:18:43.320
or some CRAN job coupled with
a programmatic deployment

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:46.770
workflow that I then
need to monitor and make

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:51.810
sure it continues to work, I
just want new data delivered

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:55.230
to my Shiny
application that users

00:18:55.230 --> 00:19:01.300
can see fresh information about
any time they visit the app.

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:06.090
So this is a great case
for adding R Markdown

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:07.560
to the equation.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.170
I call this the
division of labor.

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:15.930
Using R Markdown to create an
ETL process to put that data

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:22.740
together and then host an output
file along with that report,

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:27.420
that I can then point my
Shiny application to consume.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:33.030
So if you don't content
deployed to an end on RStudio

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:36.840
with an R Markdown
output metadata output

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:40.000
file in this case, in
the case of this image,

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:44.280
I have this report
called report title.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:45.480
Very descriptive.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:49.020
And it has an output file
that contains this data

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:50.510
that CSV files.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.580
So in the course of
this report running,

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:59.160
it creates this data that
CSV file writes it out

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:05.100
and then it just gets hosted on
RStudio Connect alongside by R

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:06.210
Markdown report.

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:10.860
So if I'm running that
report on a schedule

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:13.320
that schedule is
producing this new data

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:17.220
dot CSV file lives
at this web address

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:22.080
and then I can point Shiny to
read in that data from the URL

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:26.070
hosted on my connect server.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:28.670
So very cool.

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:33.360
And that's exactly what I want
to do for this application

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:36.660
in order to move that
work out of Shiny

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.930
and do it somewhere else
so that it doesn't have

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:43.330
to be in my Shiny code itself.

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:48.270
So this is what I built my
R Markdown Etl to generate

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.390
the same data frame
out of the making

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:55.290
calls to the trending API
and then making those R

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:58.680
best scripting function calls.

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.950
And I do this all
in an R Markdown

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:05.190
document, publish that
document to connect,

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:08.070
and then I schedule
it to run weekly

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:10.270
every Friday at a certain time.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:16.620
And I can schedule that to run
a daily or monthly or hourly,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:20.460
whatever schedule I want
to produce new data on,

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:24.120
I can do that directly
in the R Markdown

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:28.140
hosted on RStudio Connect
in the dashboard panel

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:30.220
that I'm showing here.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:36.510
So really nifty tool to
create output data files.

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:44.360
So now I have these three
pieces of content here living

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:48.650
as my production
development package.

00:21:48.650 --> 00:21:51.140
And I'm happy with the
performance of my Shiny

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:54.830
application because I've
moved that work out of Shiny

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:57.330
and into this ETL process.

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:00.470
So I have my
production assets being

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:05.600
this Etl in R Markdown, my
production application in Shiny

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:09.800
which is now fast and doesn't
repeat work for each user.

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:13.520
And then a third asset which
is not really a part of it,

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.640
but it is fun to publish
alongside as reference

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:18.590
information, which
is the Profvis

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:22.260
profile for that
production application.

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:27.050
So all of these three things can
live together and be a package

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:32.600
that I can feel confident about
running on my production server

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:38.300
and serving my
users effectively.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:41.660
So at this point, am I done?

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:44.900
Am I making an impact
at my organization

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:46.160
through what I built?

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:52.170
How do I answer that question
is what I built correct?

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:55.290
You I have these two data
products shining in R Markdown

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:59.270
and working
harmoniously together.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:02.000
Is there anything else to do?

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:06.620
So I'd like to take
a stab at trying

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:13.440
to quantify or track impact on
RStudio Connect with my assets.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.710
So how do I know
that what I've built

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:20.390
is effective at communicating
my intended message?

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:23.900
The whole goal here was
to create something useful

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:30.440
and to drive users to find
insights through this tool

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:35.120
or make changes to how we
do work at organization

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:40.410
through communicating that
in Shiny and R Markdown.

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:42.350
So am I doing that?

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:47.300
Well, there's also tools
for getting that data off

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:48.930
of your Connect server.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:53.810
And that is through usage of
another API called the RStudio

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:56.900
Connect instrumentation
data API.

00:23:56.900 --> 00:24:00.770
And this is a
really cool feature

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:04.100
that Connect has that I
think is underutilized.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:07.340
And so I want to use
this talk to encourage

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:10.040
you to look at some
of the resources

00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:15.590
we currently have for
visualizing this type of data

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:19.610
on your connect server if
you have access to a server,

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:23.900
and explore different
variations building

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:28.370
tools that can give you insights
into how much impact you're

00:24:28.370 --> 00:24:33.920
actually making through
these data products.

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:36.110
So RStudio Connect
records different types

00:24:36.110 --> 00:24:39.110
of user activity for
different types of content.

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:44.780
You've got records for Shiny
applications about each visit

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:49.140
to that application and to
the duration of that visit.

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:53.180
And then you have records
of each visit for static

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:56.510
and entered content.

00:24:56.510 --> 00:24:59.240
This dashboard you
see here on the right,

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:02.090
is one that my colleagues
on solutions that sharing

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:05.300
put together called the
Connect usage dashboard,

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:09.620
and it is available
at that GitHub URL.

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:10.880
The code is all there.

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:15.080
And we really encourage you
to take that you can drop it

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.360
into your own server
as is if you'd

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:21.290
like to see this same
information about your data

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:24.440
products, or you can
tweak it and make

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:27.500
it custom to see or
gather information that

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:29.710
will answer your own questions.

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:32.780
I've really been having a
fun time lately tweaking

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:35.510
this same
instrumentation dashboard

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:38.480
that my colleagues put together
and turning it into something

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:42.300
that I can answer questions
about individual apps.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:47.360
So this is the same dashboard
driven by the same helper

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:49.610
functions that they
created, but I've

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:53.810
tweaked them just to look at
my production application.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:58.100
And I want to know whether
this app is popular,

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:01.610
or whether this app is
just not driving traffic,

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:04.130
it's not being visited.

00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:07.310
And I want to take actions
based on the answers

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:08.370
to those questions.

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:12.380
So if this application is
popular that's awesome,

00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:15.110
I want to go to the runtime
for that application,

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:17.810
I want to evaluate
my load test and make

00:26:17.810 --> 00:26:21.650
sure that I have the right
settings for our processes

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:24.410
and connections for
process so that my user

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:27.050
base is being served there.

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:30.920
And if the application
is not driving traffic,

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:35.400
it's not getting visits,
I want this dashboard,

00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:38.720
which is an R Markdown
report to send me alert

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:43.110
and alert about that
information as well.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:47.090
This app it's not
driving the desired

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:49.230
impact to the organization.

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:54.110
So maybe I need to rethink
what's happening there.

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:59.180
And I built this application
so that you can come in and set

00:26:59.180 --> 00:27:04.220
a daily visit goal and then
set a session duration goal,

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:08.270
and you'll see here that based
on the inputs that I have

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:13.730
provided, my application
production app this CRAN Logs

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:16.850
trending R packages app
that I'm tracking here,

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:22.340
isn't hitting the total
session goal for daily count,

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:26.150
but it is hitting the average
session duration goal.

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:30.470
So one not so good,
one pretty good.

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:36.590
And these value boxes
will change color based on

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:39.750
whether or not I'm
hitting my goals.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:42.090
Behind the scenes where
you don't see here,

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:45.600
is that this report
also contains code

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:50.010
to create a custom email alert.

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:51.660
Excuse me.

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:54.490
Based on these
value boxes as well.

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:59.370
So if over time I see that I'm
just not driving the traffic

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:03.450
to my CRAN Logs
application that I wanted,

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:05.520
this report will
capture that data

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:09.435
and send based on
what it finds it

00:28:09.435 --> 00:28:14.400
will make a conditional
decision to send me

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.940
an email alert about
my underperforming app

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.260
or suppress that email.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:22.440
So in this case,
it might send me

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:26.130
an email because my
app is underperforming

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:29.880
and that email is based
on a custom template

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:33.330
that I packaged along
with my R Markdown report

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:34.840
and it looks
something like this.

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:40.780
So I can have this email
come in and say, hey,

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:45.690
for this application tile we've
seen that traffic has been

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:48.990
chronically low, you might
want to consider communicating

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.420
the same information
if it's still

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:54.390
relevant in another format.

00:28:54.390 --> 00:28:59.340
So think about whether
or not somebody is still

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:04.740
interested in interacting
with your Shiny application.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:08.640
Is this the write
method of communicating?

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:13.230
Or why is this tool not having
the impact that I want it to?

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:16.680
Sometimes web
interactivity gets old.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:19.290
Like somebody will
come to the dashboard

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:22.060
play around with
it once or twice,

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:26.460
but it's not that much fun or
interesting to click around

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:30.450
and they would rather have
that same information delivered

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:37.320
to their inbox on some schedule
so that they don't have

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.230
to go through the work of
interacting with the Shiny

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:44.220
application as funny
as that sounds.

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:47.190
Taking the steps to
go to the next server

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:50.640
and remember to
do that are steps

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:53.550
that some folks in your
organization or maybe

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:57.670
upper management are just not
going to take the time to do.

00:29:57.670 --> 00:30:04.380
And so thinking about tailoring
the experience to your audience

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:10.050
is a really important part of
that whole process when you're

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:13.200
evaluating what you
put into production

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:16.060
should continue to exist there.

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:18.600
So some things you
might want to consider

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:22.380
to sacrifice Shiny runtime
in order to achieve

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:26.520
are you can have that parameter
eStart R Markdown that we've

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:29.430
seen in previous
webinars that gets

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:33.570
scheduled sends custom
emails like this one

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:36.780
to your management.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:39.660
Scheduled email Report
Delivery I just said.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:42.780
And then a third asset
that we didn't really

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:44.970
talk too much
about in the series

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:51.090
is creating programmatic
access to communicate insights

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:54.840
through the summer package
and developing REST APIs.

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:59.010
So all of these things
are perfectly excellent

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:04.740
alternatives to creating
more Shiny interactivity,

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:09.390
but they do take effort
in order to context switch

00:31:09.390 --> 00:31:12.570
and refactor the
code that you've

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:16.260
invested time and energy
to create in producing

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:18.850
an alternative asset.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:22.770
But I think overall it's
definitely a worthwhile process

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:24.420
to go through.

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:29.310
And that brings us back to the
hierarchy of interactivity.

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:35.100
So I think my key takeaway
here is let's definitely

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.410
think about how
you want to tailor

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:42.270
your experience to your audience
and in the process of doing

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:48.540
that remember that Shiny
applications do come

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:54.480
at a cost of complexity, you do
need to iterate through Joe's

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:58.380
performance workflow in
order to move prototype

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:04.180
applications into production
ready applications.

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:07.260
And in order to get
through all of that,

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.840
I would point to these
production building

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:13.440
blocks that we have made
available in different resource

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:15.060
formats here at RStudio.

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:19.260
So again, one more
shout out to Joe Cheng

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:23.520
and to the RStudio
keynote from 2019

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:27.910
it's an excellent talk about
shining and production.

00:32:27.910 --> 00:32:32.650
And then individually
we have resources

00:32:32.650 --> 00:32:36.250
that exist on either
subdomains sites

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:42.220
that we maintain here at
RStudio or open source package

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:46.390
reference sites that are
also available on GitHub

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:51.640
and externally to the RStudio
website on code profiling

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:56.170
through Profvis, general
information on version control

00:32:56.170 --> 00:33:00.520
which is also an important
aspect of production,

